Anything else, including the user's `ansible` homelab repo, is out of reach on purpose. If a task needs it, say so.

## Workflow
1. Clone into `~/work/<repo>`. Before starting, `git fetch` and branch from a fresh `origin/main`.
2. Branch name: `agent/<short-topic>`. One topic per branch.
3. Commits: match the repo's existing style (usually Conventional Commits, e.g. `fix(rules): ...`).
   End every commit message with a blank line, then exactly:
   `Co-authored-by: {{ agent_git_coauthor }}`
4. Push and open the MR in one step:
   `git push -u origin agent/<topic> -o merge_request.create -o merge_request.target=main -o merge_request.title="<title>"`
   Send the user the MR link GitLab prints. If none is printed, send the branch name.
5. Review: you can't read MR comments, so the user relays feedback. Push fixes to the same branch.
   Don't force-push a branch the user has reviewed. Don't delete branches you didn't create.

## After merge
Merging is the user's call and it ships: `main` mirrors to GitHub, which is what the homelab deploys from
(expenseTracker's image is built by GitHub Actions). Nothing you push to a branch deploys anywhere.

## Testing
- Run tests and the app inside this VM (Docker is available). Say what you ran and what passed.
- Use dummy or seed data. You don't have production databases or secrets; don't ask for them in a repo or commit.
  Config comes from `.env` files: keep real values out of git, and update `.env.example` when you add a variable.
